       "content": {
        "id": "v2-proposal-j3-034218-root",
        "type": "question",
        "label": null,
        "prompt": "Can a triangle have the following side lengths? Use the cosine rule to decide.",
        "children": [
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j3-034218-j3-gp-a",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "$5\\text{ m}$, $6\\text{ m}$ and $13\\text{ m}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 30,
          "answer": {
           "short": "No, $\\cos\\theta=-1.8$ is less than $-1$.",
           "worked": "The largest angle $\\theta$ is opposite $13\\text{ m}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{5^2+6^2-13^2}{2(5)(6)}\\\\[8pt]&=\\frac{-108}{60}\\\\[8pt]&=-1.8\\end{align*}$$\n$\\cos\\theta<-1$, so no triangle is possible."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j3-034218-j3-gp-b",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "$6\\text{ cm}$, $9\\text{ cm}$ and $14\\text{ cm}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 30,
          "answer": {
           "short": "Yes, $\\cos\\theta=-\\frac{79}{108}$ lies between $-1$ and $1$.",
           "worked": "The largest angle $\\theta$ is opposite $14\\text{ cm}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{6^2+9^2-14^2}{2(6)(9)}\\\\[8pt]&=\\frac{-79}{108}\\end{align*}$$\n$-1<\\cos\\theta<1$, so a triangle is possible."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j3-034218-j3-gp-c",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "$2\\text{ cm}$, $3\\text{ cm}$ and $8\\text{ cm}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 30,
          "answer": {
           "short": "No, the cosine ratio for the angle opposite $2\\text{ cm}$ is $\\frac{69}{48}$, which is greater than $1$.",
           "worked": "Let $\\theta$ be the angle opposite $2\\text{ cm}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{3^2+8^2-2^2}{2(3)(8)}\\\\[8pt]&=\\frac{69}{48}\\\\[8pt]&=1.4375\\end{align*}$$\n$\\cos\\theta>1$, so no triangle is possible."
          }
         }
        ],
